% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% CHATSV - the chat room server, as a SINTRAN RT program.
%
% COMPILED AND RUN ON D100, 2026-08-18. It served a real room to two real
% clients on two terminals, and the round trip both ways is in the log:
%
%     routed letter    rcv st= 0  t= 2  len= 25   hdr ends= 16   parse k=1 nlen=5
%     ordinary msg     rcv st= 0  t= 1  len= 14   parse k=4 nlen=5   slot now= 1
%
% Those two lines came from diagnostics that have since been taken out; what
% each of them proved is written beside the code it proved it about.
%
% Every XMSG routine below, and the way it is CALLED, is taken from the routine
% reference and worked examples in
%     Operations/Cosmos/ND-60164-3-EN COSMOS Programmer Guide.md
% rather than from analogy. Four things that reference corrected, each of which
% would have cost a compile or worse:
%
%   XMPOPNM -> XMPOPCN   a room has SEATS, and the free-connection counter
%                        belongs to a CONNECTION port. With XMPOPNM the limit is
%                        silently absent while the C# side still enforces it.
%   XMPFFRE -> XMPFREL   XMPFFRE does not exist.
%   XMPFREA/XMPFWRI      take NO message identifier - they act on the CURRENT
%                        message, as XMPFSND sends it.
%   ADDR(buf)            is not enough. The guide's own examples write
%                        addr(buf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS.
%
% And every call RETURNS its status - the examples all end "=: returnStatus".
% That is how the statuses below are checked; XMOK is zero, from XMP-B02:DEFS.
%
% THE MESSAGE FORMAT IS OURS. We chose it; no ND ever sent one, so no capture
% will ever confirm it and Xmsg.Chat/ChatMessage.cs plus its tests are its only
% specification. The rules below are a transcription of Xmsg.Chat/ChatRoom.cs,
% which has the tests that say what "correct" means.
%
%     byte 0      kind          1 Join 2 Welcome 3 Reject 4 Say 5 Said
%                               6 Leave 7 Joined 8 Left 9 Rename 10 Renamed
%     byte 1      nickname length
%     bytes 2..   nickname
%     then        2 bytes text length, big endian
%     then        the text
%
% THE BYTE LAYOUT IS PINNED IN C#, and this file must agree with it by hand.
% SRC/Xmsg.Chat.Tests/ChatMessageGoldenBytesTests.cs holds the exact bytes of a
% Said, a Renamed, a Join and a 300-character line. There is no shared code
% between that and this - only those goldens - so if the layout ever changes,
% both sides change in the SAME commit or they disagree in silence.
%
%
% THREE CORRECTIONS FROM APPENDIX G, the guide's own sample CLIENT and SERVER:
%
%   flags are 2**BIT      XFWTF is a BIT POSITION (15), not a flags word. The
%                         samples both write "2**XFWTF =: FLAGS". Passing 15
%                         would set bits 0,1,2,3 - four unrelated options - and
%                         the wait would simply not happen.
%   XMMSGIDENTIFIER       the message-identifier type, with two M's. Written
%                         "XMMSGIDENTIFIER: MSGIDENT" in the sample.
%   XMUSERADDRESS         and the address idiom is
%                         ADDR(BUF(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS. ROUND brackets -
%                         the guide prints square ones and PLANC F rejects
%                         them with EXPECTS ")" ILLEGAL SYNTAX "[".
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

MODULE chatsv

% THE REAL COSMOS BINDING, from ND-10609, and INSIDE the module. Above MODULE
% the declarations land in the outer scope, the body cannot see them, and every
% call fails NOT PREVIOUSLY DECLARED while the include's own lines draw no
% diagnostic at all - so they look accepted and simply are not there.
%
% Do NOT also include XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL: it shares 184 constant names with
% XMP-B02:DEFS. DEFS is the authority for this library layer, VALUES-L for the
% raw MON 200B interface underneath it.
$INCLUDE XMP-B02:DEFS
$INCLUDE XMP-B02:IMPT

    % ----- message kinds, mirroring ChatMessageKind.cs ----------------------
    INTEGER : kJoin    := 1
    INTEGER : kWelcome := 2
    INTEGER : kReject  := 3
    INTEGER : kSay     := 4
    INTEGER : kSaid    := 5
    INTEGER : kLeave   := 6
    INTEGER : kJoined  := 7
    INTEGER : kLeft    := 8
    INTEGER : kRename  := 9
    INTEGER : kRenamed := 10

    % ONE KIND, BOTH DIRECTIONS: the member asks with an empty text, the room
    % answers with the same kind and the names in the text. See buildWho.
    INTEGER : kWho     := 11

    % THERE IS NO ELEVENTH KIND, and the reason is worth keeping.
    %
    % A kHello was invented here on 2026-08-18 to answer the connect on its own,
    % on the belief that a connection port is handed "4 bytes with byte 0 = 0"
    % and that the joiner's nickname could only follow as a second, ordinary
    % message. That belief was wrong, and it was wrong because this program was
    % reading the WRONG BYTES: the connect arrives with the XSLET letter header
    % still in front of it, and byte 0 of that header is the serial number.
    %
    % The nickname was in the very first arrival all along. See the header skip
    % in the receive loop for the layout and the manual reference. The join is
    % ONE step, exactly as the Programmer Guide describes it: the letter carries
    % the user data, the server answers it, and "thereby gives away its own
    % magic number" - the welcome IS the answer, so no extra kind is needed.

    % XFWTF is the BIT POSITION (15). The flags word is 2 to that power - the
    % sample programs in Appendix G both write "2**XFWTF =: FLAGS". Passing 15
    % itself would set bits 0 to 3, four unrelated options, and the wait would
    % not happen at all.
    % XFWTF COMES FROM XMP-B02:DEFS - do NOT declare it here. Declaring our own
    % drew IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED/DECLARED "XFWTF", and the compiler then
    % fell over with ASSERT VIOLATION AT 136747B and took the batch job with it.
    % A redeclaration is not a warning you can carry on past in this compiler.
    INTEGER : waitFlags := 0       % 2**XFWTF, computed at start

    % 2**XFSEC, computed at start. SECURE, and the reason is a measurement.
    %
    % These sends used to carry flags ZERO, and the guide is explicit about what
    % that costs: "Non-secure messages are discarded and released by XMSG if they
    % cannot be delivered." So a member whose terminal died was written to for
    % ever, in silence.
    %
    % THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE WAS TRIED FIRST AND IS REFUTED. XMPSEND is handed a
    % magic number, and a magic carries a generation, so it looked as though XMSG
    % would refuse one naming a closed port on the spot - XMXEIMA, "Invalid magic
    % number". MEASURED 2026-08-18 on D100: two clients in the room, one killed
    % with ESC ("USER BREAK AT 17202B"), the other spoke. The broadcast to the
    % dead port returned XMOK. Not one status printed, the seat stayed spent, and
    % LIST-NAMES held at 14. XMPSEND DOES NOT VALIDATE THE DESTINATION.
    %
    % So the return has to come back as a MESSAGE, which is what XFSEC buys:
    % "The message will be returned to the sending port if it cannot be
    % delivered, or if the receiving port is closed (e.g., if the receiving task
    % terminates)". It arrives as msgType XMTRE and is handled in the receive
    % loop.
    %
    % MEASURED WITH THE FLAG SET, same two clients, same kill: the bounce came
    % back carrying reason 16915 - which is XMXEIMA, the very code the refuted
    % attempt was watching for. It was never the send's status; it is the reason
    % on the return. CHAT-LOBBY went 16 free, 14 with both joined, back to 15
    % once the dead one was reaped.
    INTEGER : sendFlags := 0

    % ----- the room ---------------------------------------------------------
    % maxSeats is what XMPOPCN is given, so XROUT turns the SEVENTEENTH joiner
    % away before this program sees them. The array bounds and that argument are
    % the same number on purpose.
    %
    % THE CLIENT DOES NOT KNOW THIS NUMBER AND MUST NOT. Neither CHAT.PLNC nor
    % ChatClient.cs mentions it anywhere - XROUT does the admission itself, before
    % this program is woken, so a client that is turned away simply gets a refusal
    % it has to handle in any case. The room's size is the room's business, and
    % raising it is this file alone. The C# ChatRoom has no seat limit at all.
    %
    % Raised from 8 to 16 on 2026-08-18. Nothing else has to move with it: the
    % four arrays below and every FOR loop are written in terms of maxSeats, and
    % the only literal bounds are the array declarations, which are next to it.
    INTEGER : maxSeats := 16
    INTEGER : nameLen  := 16

    INTEGER4 ARRAY : memberMagic(1:16)

    % A two-dimensional BYTES array: sixteen names of sixteen bytes. Legal PLANC -
    % the reference gives BYTES ARRAY : b1(1:2,0:3) directly.
    %
    % Note that memberName(slot) with the LAST subscript omitted refers to the
    % WHOLE string, which is tempting for copying a name in one statement. It is
    % NOT used here, because a name is variable length and a whole-row copy would
    % carry the sixteen bytes including whatever follows the name. The explicit
    % loops are longer and right.
    % SIXTEEN BYTES PER NAME, and the number is named because the wire decides
    % how many bytes arrive. A nickname length is one byte, so a peer can ask for
    % up to 255 - and PLANC checks no array bound. Copying an unchecked length in
    % here writes straight through the other seven members' names and out the far
    % side of the array. See the guard at the top of handleMessage.
    INTEGER        : maxNameLen := 16
    % NAMES ARE UNIQUE IN THEIR FIRST TEN CHARACTERS, and that is not a style
    % note - it is what the compiler enforces. memberName and memberNameLen both
    % start MEMBERNAME, so the second silently became a redeclaration of the
    % first: IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED/DECLARED "MEMBERNAME", and then every
    % memberName(slot, i) drew MORE SUBSCRIPTS THAN IN THE ARRAY DECLARATION,
    % because the name now referred to the one-dimensional array. Four errors,
    % one cause, and none of them mention the length. The listing gives it away:
    % it prints the TRUNCATED names - BUILDRENAM, BUILDFROMS, REASONNICK.
    BYTES ARRAY    : memberName(1:16, 1:16)
    INTEGER ARRAY  : memberLen(1:16)
    INTEGER ARRAY  : memberUsed(1:16)

    % Seats whose member has VANISHED - the terminal was killed, the client was
    % aborted with ESC, the task died. Marked by sendTo when a send comes back
    % saying the port is gone, and cleared by reapDead.
    %
    % A MARK IS NOT AN EVICTION, and the separation is the whole point of this
    % array rather than a nicety. sendTo is called from inside broadcast's loop,
    % and evicting there would have to announce the departure - which means
    % building a message into outBuf while broadcast is still part way through
    % sending the outBuf it built. One shared buffer, so the message everybody
    % else is waiting for would be overwritten mid-broadcast and the room would
    % receive the departure notice instead of what was said. Marking is the only
    % thing safe to do at that depth; the acting happens back at the top.
    INTEGER ARRAY  : memberDead(1:16)

    % Is there anything to reap? Kept so the common case - nothing died - costs
    % one compare per message instead of a walk of the whole room.
    INTEGER        : deadFound := 0

    % ----- knowing when to give up ------------------------------------------
    % How many receives in a ROW have failed. Cleared by any good one, so this
    % counts a run of trouble rather than trouble over a lifetime.
    INTEGER        : badRuns := 0

    % Twenty in a row is not a bad moment, it is a broken machine. Small enough
    % that a spin is caught in under a second, large enough that a burst of
    % something transient is ridden out rather than treated as fatal.
    INTEGER        : maxBadRuns := 20

    % Print at most this many of them. The fault that made all this necessary
    % was ONE message repeated as fast as the terminal could take it, and a
    % diagnostic with no limit buries the line that says what happened.
    INTEGER        : maxNoisy := 5

    INTEGER ARRAY : stack(0:1000)

    % SIZE OF inBuf, named because the read must clamp against it. XMSG reports
    % the SENDER'S message size; nothing stops that being larger than this
    % array, and PLANC checks no bound.
    INTEGER : inBufSize := 256
    BYTES : inBuf(0:255)
    BYTES : outBuf(0:255)

    % ONE SPACE, AS A BYTE. buildWho puts a separator between names, and
    % "' ' =: outBuf(at)" does not compile: 'x' is a STRING and an element of a
    % BYTES array is a BYTE. Held as a one-element BYTES so the copy is
    % byte-to-byte.
    BYTES : spaceByte := ' '

    INTEGER  : myPort
    INTEGER  : msgType

    % XMROU, "routed message (via XROUT)", is what msgType carries when a letter
    % arrives. It is the ONLY thing that says a SEAT was spent: XROUT takes one
    % free connection to forward a letter, before this program has seen a byte
    % of it.
    %
    % SETTLED FROM ND'S OWN SAMPLE SERVERS, both of them, in the COSMOS
    % Programmer Guide (ND-60.164.3) appendix of sample programs. Each waits for
    % the forwarded letter and then refuses anything else:
    %
    %   PLANC     IF MSGTYPE >< XMROU THEN
    %                 OUTPUT(1, 'A', 'WRONG MESSAGE TYPE ')
    %
    %   FORTRAN   IF (MSGTYPE .NE. XMROU) THEN
    %                 WRITE(1,*)'WRONG MESSAGE TYPE'
    %
    % Both then read the CLIENT's magic number out of the arrived message to
    % answer it directly - which is the other half of the same fact, and the
    % reason the sender field is the client rather than XROUT.
    %
    % The name is used bare here, not declared as a literal, because the PLANC
    % sample uses it bare under the same two includes this file has.

    % Was the sender in the room BEFORE this message was handled? The seat
    % accounting below needs the answer from before, so it is taken early.
    INTEGER  : wasMember
    % Is the sender a member AFTER the letter was handled? That single question
    % decides whether the seat XROUT spent on the arrival goes back.
    INTEGER  : nowMember
    % Byte 1 of a routed arrival, kept BEFORE the payload is shifted down. 65
    % (XSLET) means a client sent it and a seat was spent; anything else is
    % XROUT answering one of our own calls, which spent nothing.
    INTEGER  : letterService

    % XSLET (65) IS NOT DECLARED HERE. It is already a CONSTANT in XMP-B02:DEFS
    % - "CONSTANT XSLET=65  % Send a letter" - and declaring our own would be a
    % redeclaration, which in this compiler is not a warning: it drew
    % IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED and then ASSERT VIOLATION AT 136747B and took
    % the whole batch job with it. Checked against the listing before writing
    % this, not assumed.
    INTEGER  : remotePortHash
    INTEGER  : nBytes
    % What XMPFRCV put in nBytes, kept before XMPFMST overwrites it. It is the
    % message length normally and the REASON FOR RETURN on an XMTRE.
    INTEGER  : receiveStat
    INTEGER  : readLength
    INTEGER  : readAmount
    % An INTEGER dumpAmount clamped the 48-byte arrival dump. Both are gone;
    % see the note in the receive loop for what that dump found.
    % Where the chat message starts inside a letter that came via XROUT - that
    % is, the first byte after the XSLET header. Derived from the header's own
    % length word on every arrival, never assumed.
    INTEGER  : payloadAt
    INTEGER  : writtenLength
    INTEGER  : returnStatus
    INTEGER4 : senderMagic

    % OUR OWN port's magic number, read once with XMPFP2M after the port opens.
    % Kept only so an arrival can be tested against it, because the thing that
    % actually happened - our own answer arriving back at this very port - is not
    % something any amount of reading the guide would have suggested, and without
    % this comparison it cannot be told apart from a real peer's message.
    INTEGER4 : myMagic := 0
    BOOLEAN  : uniqueName := TRUE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Compare a name in the room against one in a buffer, ignoring case.
    %
    % ChatRoom.cs compares ignoring case, so this does too - the two doors into
    % one room must not disagree about whether ANNA and anna are the same
    % person.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : sameName(slot, at, length)
        INTEGER : j, a, b
        IF memberLen(slot) >< length THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR j IN 1:length DO
            memberName(slot, j) =: a
            inBuf(at + j - 1) =: b
            IF a > 96 THEN a - 32 =: a ENDIF        % fold lower case to upper
            IF b > 96 THEN b - 32 =: b ENDIF
            IF a >< b THEN
                0 RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        1 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER4) : findByMagic(magic)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF memberMagic(i) = magic THEN
                    i RETURN
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : findByName(at, length)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF sameName(i, at, length) = 1 THEN
                    i RETURN
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : freeSlot
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) = 0 THEN
                i RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build a message into outBuf. Returns its total length.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            buildMessage(kind, nameAt, nameLength, textAt, textLength)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kind =: outBuf(0)
        nameLength =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
            inBuf(nameAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        0 =: outBuf(at)                       % text length, high byte
        textLength =: outBuf(at + 1)          % text length, low byte
        at + 2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
            inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build the answer to /who: every seated member's name, one space between.
    %
    % The name field is EMPTY - the room is answering, not a member - and the
    % names all go in the text. One message, not one per member: a message each
    % would multiply the scarcest thing XMSG has by the size of the room. The
    % measurement is in DOC/BRINGUP-ORDER-AND-TRAPS-2026-08-18.md: "Data
    % transmit blocks" has a limit of TEN and a 20-user burst hits it.
    %
    % BOUNDED, AND THE BOUND IS NOT DECORATION. outBuf holds 256 bytes and the
    % header eats four, so 252 are left; sixteen seats of sixteen characters
    % plus separators come to 271. A full room of long names would therefore
    % write past the end of outBuf, and PLANC checks no array bound - it would
    % simply corrupt whatever the loader put next. Names that do not fit are
    % dropped, which is a worse answer than a longer buffer would give and a
    % far better one than a corrupted program.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : buildWho
        INTEGER : i, j, at, textAt, textLen, room

        kWho =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                        % no name: the ROOM is answering
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)                       % text length, high byte
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)                   % text length, low byte, filled in below
        at + 2 =: textAt
        textAt =: at
        0 =: textLen

        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) = 1 THEN
                % What this name needs: a separator if it is not the first,
                % then the name itself.
                memberLen(i) =: room
                IF textLen > 0 THEN
                    room + 1 =: room
                ENDIF

                IF textLen + room <= 250 THEN
                    IF textLen > 0 THEN
                        % A BYTE FROM A BYTES ARRAY, not a string literal.
                        % "' ' =: outBuf(at)" is what stood here and the
                        % compiler answered
                        %   1082 (422)/BUILDWHO *** ERROR
                        %        - ILLEGAL DATA TYPE "OUTBUF"
                        % because 'x' is a STRING and outBuf(at) is a BYTE.
                        % Copying out of a one-byte BYTES gives the element the
                        % type it wants - the same move oneChar uses on the
                        % client side for printing.
                        spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                        textLen + 1 =: textLen
                    ENDIF
                    FOR j IN 1:memberLen(i) DO
                        memberName(i, j) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                    ENDFOR
                    textLen + memberLen(i) =: textLen
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        % Now the length is known. It cannot exceed 250, so the high byte the
        % format carries stays zero.
        textLen =: outBuf(3)
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build a message whose name comes from a member slot rather than from the
    % incoming buffer - used for Said, where the speaker is named by the room
    % and NOT by whatever the sender claimed.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            buildFromSlot(kind, slot, textAt, textLength)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kind =: outBuf(0)
        memberLen(slot) =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:memberLen(slot) DO
            memberName(slot, i) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        textLength =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
            inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Copy a name out of the incoming buffer into a member slot.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : copyNameIn(slot, at, length)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:length DO
            inBuf(at + i - 1) =: memberName(slot, i)
        ENDFOR
        length =: memberLen(slot)
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build the Renamed announcement. Returns its total length.
    %
    % The NEW name goes in the name field and the OLD one in the text. That is
    % not a choice made here - ChatMessageKind.cs states it, and the reason is
    % that everybody has a transcript on screen under the old name and no way to
    % connect the two without it. CALL THIS BEFORE the slot is changed, while
    % the old name is still in it.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            buildRenamed(slot, newAt, newLength)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kRenamed =: outBuf(0)
        newLength =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:newLength DO
            inBuf(newAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        % The old name as the text.
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        memberLen(slot) =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:memberLen(slot) DO
            memberName(slot, i) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Send outBuf to one magic number.
    %
    % XMPFGET reserves a buffer and makes it CURRENT; XMPFWRI writes into the
    % current message; XMPFSND sends it. Note the userAddress coercion - the
    % guide's own examples write ADDR(buf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER4, INTEGER) : sendTo(magic, length)
        XMMSGIDENTIFIER : ident
        INTEGER : deadSlot
        % Each of the three steps below printed its status on EVERY send while
        % the room was being brought up - three lines per message, per member.
        % They now speak only when a step fails, which is the case that was
        % actually worth seeing: a send that half-succeeds is how a member ends
        % up in the room and hearing nothing.
        xmpfget(0, length, ident) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', 'SV: get failed st=')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        xmpfwri(0, 0, &
            ADDR(outBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
            0, length, writtenLength) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', 'SV: wri failed st=')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
        ENDIF
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            xmpfrel(0, ident) =: returnStatus
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        % XMPSEND, NOT XMPFSND - and this is the whole bug, not a tidy-up.
        %
        % XMPFSND takes no message parameter. The guide says which one it picks:
        % "the current (default) message buffer is assumed, namely the 'port
        % current' message if one exists, or, if none, the 'task current'
        % message" (ND-60.164.3, XMPFSND, EXPLANATION). XMPFGET above only makes
        % the new buffer TASK current - it has no port parameter and cannot make
        % it port current. This port has just received a message, so a 'port
        % current' message may well still exist, and XMPFSND would send THAT
        % instead of the reply we just built. The guide never says whether
        % XMPFREL clears the port-current pointer, so relying on it would be a
        % guess.
        %
        % XMPSEND("Send message, not necessarily current") removes the question:
        % "The message specified by msgIdentifier is set as 'task current'
        % message and as 'port current' message for localPort. Then the message
        % will be sent as described under routine XMPFSND."
        %
        % Appendix G's SERVER gets away with XMPFSND only because it never calls
        % XMPFGET - it replies in the very buffer that arrived, which is already
        % both port and task current. We cannot copy that: a broadcast needs one
        % fresh buffer per member.
        xmpsend(sendFlags, ident, myPort, magic) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', 'SV: snd failed st=')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')

            % ---- A MEMBER WHO IS NO LONGER THERE, IF WE ARE EVER TOLD HERE --
            % Two statuses would mean the seat is holding somebody who has gone.
            % Both are read off XMP-B02:DEFS rather than guessed:
            %
            %   XMXEIMA 16915  Invalid magic number
            %   XMXEPCL 16932  Remote port closed while message queued
            %
            % NEITHER OF THEM ACTUALLY TURNS UP for a client killed on this
            % machine - measured, see the note on sendFlags. XMPSEND took the
            % message for a closed port and answered XMOK. This test is kept
            % rather than deleted because it costs one compare and it is right
            % if a status ever does arrive - a remote member across a link is
            % the obvious untested case - but it is NOT what fixes the leak.
            % The XMTRE branch in the receive loop is.
            %
            % ONLY THESE TWO, and deliberately not "any failure". XMXEMFL
            % (message space full) is a bad moment, not a dead member, and
            % throwing somebody out of the room because the machine was briefly
            % short of buffers would be worse than the bug being fixed. Every
            % other status still prints above.
            IF returnStatus = XMXEIMA OR returnStatus = XMXEPCL THEN
                findByMagic(magic) =: deadSlot
                IF deadSlot >< 0 THEN
                    1 =: memberDead(deadSlot)
                    1 =: deadFound
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : broadcast(length, exceptSlot)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF i >< exceptSlot THEN
                    sendTo(memberMagic(i), length)
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Empty the seats of members who have vanished, and give each seat back.
    %
    % CALLED FROM THE TOP OF THE RECEIVE LOOP, never from inside a broadcast -
    % see the note on memberDead for why that is not a style choice.
    %
    % A vanished member costs TWO things and they are not the same thing:
    %
    %   the seat in this program  memberUsed, which decides who is in the room;
    %   the seat in XROUT         the free-connection counter, which decides
    %                             whether a new joiner's letter is forwarded at
    %                             all. XROUT decremented it to deliver the join
    %                             and only the port's owner - this program -
    %                             ever puts one back, with XMPINFC.
    %
    % Clearing only the first leaves the room looking empty in LIST-NAMES while
    % it refuses everybody. MEASURED 2026-08-18, before this routine existed:
    % CHAT-LOBBY went 16 free to 15 when a client was aborted with ESC
    % ("USER BREAK AT 16665B") and stayed at 15 for good.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : reapDead
        INTEGER : round, i, length

        % Cleared FIRST, not last. A broadcast below can discover another dead
        % member - telling the room somebody left is exactly when the next dead
        % port gets found - and clearing at the end would throw that mark away.
        % Cleared here, a mark raised during our own broadcasts simply re-arms
        % this routine for the next message.
        0 =: deadFound

        % Bounded rather than a WHILE. Each pass empties at least one seat and a
        % pass can only mark members still in the room, so maxSeats passes empty
        % the worst room there is. An unbounded loop here would be taking on
        % trust that a broadcast to a dying room always terminates, and this
        % program has no way to recover if it does not.
        FOR round IN 1:maxSeats DO
            FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
                IF memberDead(i) >< 0 THEN
                    0 =: memberDead(i)
                    IF memberUsed(i) >< 0 THEN
                        % Same order as the kLeave path: build the announcement
                        % while the name is still in the slot, empty the seat,
                        % THEN tell the room - so the departing member is not
                        % sent their own Left notice at a port already gone,
                        % which would only mark them dead all over again.
                        buildFromSlot(kLeft, i, 0, 0) =: length
                        0 =: memberUsed(i)
                        broadcast(length, 0)

                        xmpinfc(0, myPort, 1, 0) =: returnStatus
                        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
                            OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', 'SV: infc failed st')
                            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                        ENDIF

                        % Worth one line: an operator watching the room needs to
                        % be able to tell "they left" from "they died", because
                        % only the second is evidence of anything.
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL21', 'SV: reaped dead slot ')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', i)
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % ----- the refusal reasons, worded exactly as ChatRoom.cs words them ----
    % Carried on the wire so a client shows the same sentence whichever door it
    % came in by. If these and ChatRoom ever diverge, one of the two is wrong.
    %
    % EACH LENGTH IS WRITTEN OUT beside its string, and that is deliberate. An
    % earlier version called LENGTH(reason) - PLANC has no such routine. The one
    % it does have is SIZE, whose syntax is "SIZE ident =: var" with no
    % parentheses, and which returns the STORAGE size; what that means for a
    % BYTES value passed as a routine parameter is not established. Passing the
    % count removes the question rather than trading one guess for another.
    %
    % The cost is that a string and its number must be changed together. That is
    % a real risk and it is why they are on adjacent lines.
    BYTES : reasonNeedNick := 'a nickname is required'
    INTEGER : lenNeedNick := 22
    BYTES : reasonNickTaken    := 'that nickname is taken'
    INTEGER : lenNickTaken := 22
    BYTES : reasonAlreadyIn    := 'already joined'
    INTEGER : lenAlreadyIn := 14
    BYTES : reasonRoomFull     := 'the room is full'
    INTEGER : lenRoomFull := 16

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Refuse the sender, echoing the name they asked for and naming the reason.
    %
    % The name is echoed so the asker can tell WHICH request failed when it has
    % more than one outstanding; the reason is carried so the wording is the
    % same on both doors.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER) : &
            refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reason, reasonLength)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kReject =: outBuf(0)
        nameLength =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
            inBuf(nameAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        0 =: outBuf(at)
        reasonLength =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        % SUBSCRIPT FROM ZERO. A BYTES declared from a literal - which is what
        % every reason string above is - has its first character at index 0, so
        % "reason(i)" for i in 1:len silently drops the first letter and reads
        % one byte past the end. Same defect, same day, in CHAT.PLNC's buildOwn,
        % where it turned RONNY into ONNY on the wire and was only visible
        % because the raw bytes were dumped. Nothing warns; PLANC checks no
        % bound. Compare cmdIs in CHAT.PLNC, which has always used word(i - 1).
        FOR i IN 1:reasonLength DO
            reason(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        sendTo(senderMagic, at)
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Decode one arrived message and apply the room rules.
    %
    % A transcription of Xmsg.Chat/ChatRoom.cs. Where that file refuses, this
    % refuses for the same reason and in the same order, because the two doors
    % lead into one room.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : handleMessage
        INTEGER : kind, nameLength, nameAt, textLength, textAt
        INTEGER : slot, taken, length

        inBuf(0) =: kind
        inBuf(1) =: nameLength
        2 =: nameAt

        % A "parse k= nlen= rlen=" line printed here on EVERY message while the
        % join was being brought up. It said "remove once the join works", and
        % the join works - it was what showed the kind arriving as the XSLET
        % serial number instead of kJoin, which is how the missing header skip
        % was found.
        %
        % THE THREE "DROP" MESSAGES BELOW STAY. They are not the same thing: a
        % silent drop is right for a stranger on the wire but leaves the operator
        % unable to tell a refused message from a lost one, and each prints only
        % when something is actually wrong rather than once per message.

        % ---- TRUST NOTHING THAT ARRIVED ------------------------------------
        % Every field below is a byte a stranger chose. Two things have to hold
        % before any of them is used, and neither is checked by the language:
        %
        %   the name must FIT      memberName has maxNameLen bytes per seat, and
        %                          a length byte can say 255.
        %   the fields must be IN  the message actually read is readLength
        %   THE MESSAGE            bytes; a length that points past it would read
        %                          whatever the previous, longer message left in
        %                          inBuf and answer with it.
        %
        % Dropped in silence rather than refused: a refusal would need a name to
        % echo, and the name is the part that is not trustworthy. The seat is
        % settled by the receive loop either way, which is exactly why that
        % accounting does not live in here.
        %
        % The sixteen is a rule of the ROOM, not a detail of this program:
        % ChatRoom.MaxNicknameLength says the same number, so a name this side
        % must drop is one the other side refuses rather than admits. Otherwise
        % the two rooms would disagree about who is in them.
        IF nameLength > maxNameLen THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL21', 'SV: DROP name too big')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF nameAt + nameLength + 1 > readLength THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'SV: DROP name past end')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        nameAt + nameLength =: textAt
        inBuf(textAt) * 256 + inBuf(textAt + 1) =: textLength
        textAt + 2 =: textAt

        IF textAt + textLength > readLength THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'SV: DROP text past end')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % The seat this sender already holds, or -1 if none. A "slot now=" line
        % used to print here on every message; it was part of the same bring-up
        % as the parse line above and went with it.
        findByMagic(senderMagic) =: slot

        IF kind = kJoin THEN
            % Order matches ChatRoom.TryJoin: empty name, then taken name, then
            % already joined.
            IF nameLength = 0 THEN
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonNeedNick, lenNeedNick)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            findByName(nameAt, nameLength) =: taken
            IF taken >< 0 THEN
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonNickTaken, lenNickTaken)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF slot >< 0 THEN
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonAlreadyIn, lenAlreadyIn)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            freeSlot =: slot
            IF slot = 0 THEN
                % XROUT should have stopped this - the seat count it holds and
                % maxSeats here are the same number. If it ever happens, the
                % two have drifted apart and that is worth refusing loudly
                % rather than overwriting a member.
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonRoomFull, lenRoomFull)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            1 =: memberUsed(slot)
            senderMagic =: memberMagic(slot)
            copyNameIn(slot, nameAt, nameLength)

            % The welcome goes only to the joiner; the room hears Joined.
            buildMessage(kWelcome, nameAt, nameLength, 0, 0) =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)

            buildMessage(kJoined, nameAt, nameLength, 0, 0) =: length
            broadcast(length, slot)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % Everything below requires membership. Silence for a stranger, exactly
        % as ChatRoom does: answering would confirm the port is a chat server to
        % something that never joined.
        IF slot = 0 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kSay THEN
            % The speaker is named by the ROOM, never by what the sender put in
            % the message - otherwise anyone could speak as anyone.
            buildFromSlot(kSaid, slot, textAt, textLength) =: length
            broadcast(length, 0)                  % 0 = everybody, speaker too
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kRename THEN
            IF nameLength = 0 THEN
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonNeedNick, lenNeedNick)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF sameName(slot, nameAt, nameLength) = 1 THEN
                RETURN                            % already your name: not news
            ENDIF
            findByName(nameAt, nameLength) =: taken
            IF taken >< 0 THEN
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonNickTaken, lenNickTaken)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % Renamed carries the NEW name and the OLD one as its text, so a
            % transcript on somebody's screen can be connected up. Built before
            % the slot changes, while the old name is still there.
            buildRenamed(slot, nameAt, nameLength) =: length
            copyNameIn(slot, nameAt, nameLength)
            broadcast(length, 0)                  % including the asker
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % /who - answered to the ASKER ALONE, never broadcast. Everybody else
        % asking would then get a list they did not want, and the room would
        % spend a message on each of them.
        IF kind = kWho THEN
            buildWho =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kLeave THEN
            buildFromSlot(kLeft, slot, 0, 0) =: length
            0 =: memberUsed(slot)
            broadcast(length, 0)

            % Give the seat back to XROUT, or the room fills up permanently and
            % XROUT stops forwarding joins long after anybody left.
            xmpinfc(0, myPort, 1, 0) =: returnStatus
            RETURN
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % The program.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    PROGRAM : chatServer
        XMMSGIDENTIFIER : msgIdent
        INTEGER : i

        INISTACK stack

        % The flags WORDS from their bit positions - see the note at the top.
        2**XFWTF =: waitFlags
        2**XFSEC =: sendFlags

        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            0 =: memberUsed(i)
            0 =: memberDead(i)
        ENDFOR

        % A CONNECTION port: XROUT holds the free-connection counter and turns
        % the joiner past maxSeats away for us, before this program is woken.
        % That is the whole reason no client knows the number.
        xmpopcn(0, 'CHAT-LOBBY', uniqueName, maxSeats, myPort) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL29', 'CHATSV: cannot open the room$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL11', 'SV: opn st=')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % Our own magic number, so an arrival can be tested against it.
        xmpfp2m(0, myPort, myMagic) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', 'SV: p2m failed st=')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
        ENDIF

        % ONE BANNER, AND IT STAYS. An operator has to be able to tell a server
        % that is waiting from one that never got its room, and the port number
        % is what LIST-PORTS is matched against when it does not.
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', 'CHATSV: room open, port ')
        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', myPort)
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')

        DO
            % WAIT. XFWTF is what makes this block rather than spin; an RT
            % program that polls burns the machine for nothing. Without the
            % flag the status would come back MXNEXTM when nothing is waiting.
            xmpfrcv(waitFlags, myPort, msgType, remotePortHash, msgIdent, nBytes) &
                =: returnStatus
            % A RECEIVE IS A MESSAGE ONLY WHEN THE STATUS IS A MESSAGE TYPE.
            % Not "= XMOK" and not "> 0". The library remaps statuses: 0 is OK
            % for other calls but is NOT a message type, so comparing to it
            % here discards every message; and 16896 (XMXENTM, nothing there)
            % and every error are positive, so "> 0" accepts an empty port.
            % The constants file settles it - "Message types: Returned as
            % successfull status from XFRCV", XMTNO=1 through XMTPS=6.
            % PRINT THE STATUS OF EVERY RECEIVE, OUTSIDE THE TEST.
            %
            % This line is here because the test below threw the message away
            % and every diagnostic was INSIDE it, so the server looked as if it
            % had never woken at all. MEASURED 2026-08-18: CHAT-LOBBY went from
            % 8 free seats to 6 across two joins, which proves XROUT accepted
            % both letters and forwarded them here - "XROUT will decrement the
            % counter and forward the whole message to portName" - while this
            % program printed nothing and went straight back to waiting.
            %
            % A gate must never be the only thing that can tell you it closed.
            % This printed on EVERY receive during bring-up. It now prints only
            % when the receive is NOT one this server accepts - which is the case
            % the original comment was really about. A message that arrives and is
            % handled says so by what it does; a message thrown away by the test
            % below still has nothing else to speak for it.
            % ---- KNOW WHEN TO STOP -------------------------------------
            % MEASURED 2026-08-18, and it is why this block exists at all.
            % XMSG went down under a running server. XMPFRCV then stopped
            % blocking - there is nothing to wait ON - and returned 16933,
            % XMXENRU, "XMSG not running", instantly, for ever. This loop
            % printed that line as fast as the terminal could take it and
            % burned the machine until somebody pressed ESC.
            %
            % A WAIT THAT CANNOT WAIT IS A SPIN. The XFWTF flag is what makes
            % this loop cheap, and it quietly stops meaning anything the moment
            % the thing it waits on is gone. Any program built around a
            % blocking call needs an answer for that, and "print and go round
            % again" is not one.
            %
            % Two ways out, and they are different on purpose:
            %
            %   XMXENRU        STOP. Nothing this program can do brings XMSG
            %                  back - an operator has to restart it - so
            %                  retrying is not persistence, it is a busy loop
            %                  with no end. Say so plainly and leave.
            %
            %   anything else  RETRY, but not for ever. An unknown status may
            %                  well clear; the same one arriving maxBadRuns
            %                  times running has not cleared and will not.
            %
            % A GOOD RECEIVE CLEARS THE COUNT, so the limit is on a RUN of
            % failures rather than on failures for the life of the program. A
            % server that has been up for a week and sees twenty scattered odd
            % statuses is healthy; one that sees twenty in a row is not.
            IF returnStatus = XMOK OR &
               (returnStatus >= XMTNO AND returnStatus <= XMTPS) THEN
                0 =: badRuns
            ELSE
                IF returnStatus >< XMXENTM THEN
                    badRuns + 1 =: badRuns

                    % Print the first few and then stop shouting. The whole
                    % fault above was a message repeated without limit, and a
                    % diagnostic that fills the screen hides the one line that
                    % matters.
                    IF badRuns <= maxNoisy THEN
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL23', 'SV: receive refused st=')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                    ENDIF

                    IF returnStatus = XMXENRU THEN
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL31', 'CHATSV: XMSG is not running - $')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL31', 'CHATSV: stopping, restart XMSG$')
                        RETURN
                    ENDIF

                    IF badRuns > maxBadRuns THEN
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL30', 'CHATSV: too many bad receives$')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                        RETURN
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF

            % ACCEPT XMOK TOO - Appendix G's SERVER does exactly this.
            %
            % The old test was the range XMTNO..XMTPS alone, defended by a
            % comment claiming 0 "is NOT a message type, so comparing to it here
            % discards every message". The vendor sample says otherwise: after
            % XMPFRCV it tests "IF RETURNSTATUS >< XMOK THEN ... bad status" and
            % only THEN looks at the MSGTYPE out-parameter to check for XMROU.
            % So XMOK is a successful receive, and the message type belongs in
            % msgType, not in the status.
            %
            % Both forms are accepted here rather than one being swapped for the
            % other, because the print above will say which one actually turns up
            % and guessing is what put the range test here in the first place.
            IF returnStatus = XMOK OR &
               (returnStatus >= XMTNO AND returnStatus <= XMTPS) THEN
                % The FULL magic number of the sender - XMPFRCV hands back only
                % a hashed port, and we must be able to answer.
                % A "got t= len=" line printed here to separate "never woke"
                % from "woke, replied, reply lost". It said to remove it once the
                % join works, and the join works.
                % THE RECEIVE'S OWN nBytes, KEPT BEFORE ANYTHING OVERWRITES IT.
                % For an ordinary message it is the length; for a RETURNED one
                % the guide says it is something else entirely - "If msgType is
                % XMTRE, msgLengthOrStat contains the error status" - and
                % xmpfmst below writes over it either way.
                nBytes =: receiveStat

                xmpfmst(0, msgIdent, msgType, senderMagic, nBytes) =: returnStatus

                % CLAMP BEFORE READING. nBytes is the sender's message size,
                % handed back by XMSG - not a size this program chose. Passing
                % it straight in lets any peer that sends more than inBufSize
                % bytes write past the end of inBuf. That is a WRITE overflow
                % and it happens before a single field is parsed, so none of
                % the careful field checks in handleMessage can save it.
                nBytes =: readAmount
                IF readAmount > inBufSize THEN
                    inBufSize =: readAmount
                ENDIF

                xmpfrea(0, 0, &
                    ADDR(inBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
                    0, readAmount, readLength) =: returnStatus

                xmpfrel(0, msgIdent) =: returnStatus

                % A 48-BYTE RAW DUMP OF EVERY ARRIVAL STOOD HERE, and it is the
                % single diagnostic that solved this program. It is worth saying
                % what it did before it goes, because the lesson outlives it.
                %
                % It began at 8 bytes and printed nothing useful for the arrival
                % that mattered. Widened to 48 it showed the join in full:
                %
                %   123  65  0 12 | 255 10 'CHAT-LOBBY' | 1 5 'RONNY' 0 0
                %
                % Byte 0 was 123 - the SERIAL NUMBER this program's own client
                % hands to XMPBLET. So what arrives starts with the XSLET LETTER
                % HEADER, and handleMessage had been reading that header as if it
                % were a chat message all along. The header skip below is the fix,
                % and the layout under it is read off those bytes.
                %
                % The general form: a dump of what actually arrived beat every
                % re-reading of the code, and it beat the manual too - the manual
                % was right and was being applied to the wrong offset.

                % A "fromSelf YES/no" line printed here. It earned its place once:
                % the answer came back YES, the server's own reply arriving
                % straight back at this port, which no amount of reading the guide
                % would have suggested. That is recorded rather than printed now.
                % THE SENDER'S MAGIC NUMBER AND OUR OWN were printed side by side
                % here, to answer one suspicion: the welcome was being sent with
                % status 0 and never arriving, so was senderMagic - taken from
                % XMPFMST on a letter that came via XROUT - an address we could
                % actually send BACK to?
                %
                % IT WAS. The two numbers were consistent and the reply address
                % was never the bug. What that ruled out is what sent the search
                % to the CLIENT's receive test, where XMPFRCV was returning XMOK,
                % a "1..6 only" test was rejecting it, and the welcome was being
                % dequeued and dropped in silence.
                %
                % A diagnostic that clears a suspicion is worth as much as one
                % that confirms it, and this one cost a push-and-compile round
                % trip to add. That is why it is written down instead of deleted.

                % ---- A LETTER ARRIVES WEARING ITS XROUT HEADER --------------
                % XROUT forwards THE WHOLE MESSAGE, header included. So a letter
                % that came via XROUT has the XSLET header in front of the chat
                % message, and the chat message has to be found behind it.
                %
                % ND-60.164.3 EN COSMOS Programmer Guide, appendix B, "XROUT
                % Message Format", states the layout - this is read from the
                % manual, not inferred from one capture:
                %
                %   byte 0     serial number, echoed back unchanged by XROUT
                %   byte 1     the service number (XSLET = 65); XROUT overwrites
                %              it with the return status on a reply
                %   bytes 2-3  LENGTH OF THE REMAINDER OF THE MESSAGE in bytes,
                %              "followed by a sequence of parameter blocks"
                %
                % So the parameter blocks occupy exactly the bytes that length
                % counts, and OUR data starts right after them:
                %
                %   payloadAt = 4 + the length word
                %
                % The manual is explicit that the user data is untouched: "The
                % remainder of the message will not be looked at by XROUT ... thus
                % the user data can be coded in any format legible to the
                % receiving (server) task."
                %
                % MEASURED 2026-08-18, the 25-byte join, which agrees:
                %   123  65  0 12 | 255 10 'CHAT-LOBBY' | 1 5 'RONNY' 0 0
                %    ^serial ^len=12   ^param 1: string, 10 bytes  ^payload at 16
                %
                % DERIVED, NEVER HARD-CODED. 16 is only right for a ten-character
                % room name; the parameter block moves with the name, and a
                % constant here would break the moment /join names a longer room.
                %
                % The alternative reading - 6 + the name length - fits this one
                % capture just as well and is WRONG in general: it cannot see a
                % second parameter block, and XSLET has an optional third.
                %
                % The payload is slid down to offset 0 so that handleMessage
                % parses one layout and one only. Every other route into it -
                % an ordinary XMTNO message straight from a magic number - has no
                % header at all, and this is the single place that knows that.
                % ---- A MESSAGE OF OURS THAT COULD NOT BE DELIVERED ----------
                % This is what XFSEC bought, and it has to be caught BEFORE
                % handleMessage: the bytes in inBuf are our own outgoing Said or
                % Joined coming home, so parsing them as an arrival would have
                % the room answering itself.
                %
                % THE QUESTION THIS BRANCH WAS BUILT TO ANSWER, AND THE ANSWER.
                %
                % A returned message carries what WE wrote, and what we wrote
                % names the SPEAKER, not the member it could not reach - so it
                % was not obvious that anything here identifies the destination.
                % XMPFMST hands back a magic number for an ordinary arrival; the
                % guide never says what that field holds on a returned one.
                %
                % MEASURED 2026-08-18 on D100, and it IS the destination:
                %
                %   SV: returned msg, st=  16915
                %   SV: it names slot          2
                %   SV: reaped dead slot       2
                %
                % 16915 is XMXEIMA, "Invalid magic number" - the reason the
                % message came home. Note where that code turned up: as the
                % REASON ON A RETURN, not as the status of the send. The first
                % attempt at this fix tested XMPSEND's own status for exactly
                % that number and never fired, because XMPSEND answered XMOK and
                % handed the message to a port that was already gone.
                %
                % The slot print stays. It costs nothing - a member has to die
                % before it runs - and it separates "one of ours bounced" from
                % "something bounced", which is the first thing an operator
                % needs to know and the one thing the reap line cannot say.
                IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'SV: returned msg, st= ')
                    OUTPUT(1, 'I6', receiveStat)
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')

                    findByMagic(senderMagic) =: wasMember
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'SV: it names slot     ')
                    OUTPUT(1, 'I6', wasMember)
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')

                    IF wasMember >< 0 THEN
                        1 =: memberDead(wasMember)
                        1 =: deadFound
                    ENDIF
                ELSE
                IF msgType = XMROU THEN
                    % THE SERVICE BYTE, TAKEN BEFORE ANYTHING MOVES. The payload
                    % is slid down to offset 0 below, so by the time the seat
                    % rule runs inBuf(1) is chat data, not the header. Reading
                    % it there is how the feedback loop went unnoticed in
                    % review - the test looked right and was inspecting the
                    % wrong bytes.
                    inBuf(1) =: letterService

                    4 + inBuf(2) * 256 + inBuf(3) =: payloadAt

                    % A letter with nothing behind the header is a connect this
                    % program has no answer for. Dropping it says so; the seat is
                    % already spent either way. Note this is >=, not >: a payload
                    % that starts exactly AT the end is an empty payload.
                    IF payloadAt >= readLength THEN
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', 'SV: DROP empty letter   ')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                    ELSE
                        % (the seat for this arrival is settled after
                        %  handleMessage, by the one rule below)
                        readLength - payloadAt =: readLength
                        FOR i IN 1:readLength DO
                            inBuf(payloadAt + i - 1) =: inBuf(i - 1)
                        ENDFOR

                        % ---- SEAT ACCOUNTING, and it belongs HERE ----------
                        % The seat is spent by the ARRIVAL of a routed message,
                        % not by any particular kind of message inside it. Doing
                        % it per kind is what the C# side got wrong: a letter
                        % carrying anything but a join, or a letter too damaged
                        % to decode at all, never reaches a handler - so no
                        % handler can give its seat back, and the room silently
                        % loses a seat for good.
                        %
                        % XROUT decremented its free-connection counter to get
                        % this letter here, so a seat is already spent by the
                        % time this line runs. There are now exactly two places
                        % that give one back, and between them they cover both
                        % ways a member can stop being one:
                        %
                        %   handleMessage, kLeave   they said goodbye;
                        %   reapDead                they did not, and a send to
                        %                           them came back saying the
                        %                           port is gone.
                        findByMagic(senderMagic) =: wasMember

                        handleMessage
                    ENDIF

                    % ---- THE SEAT BELONGS TO THE ARRIVAL, SO SETTLE IT HERE ----
                    % MEASURED 2026-08-18 by a load run: twenty C# clients joined
                    % at once and CHAT-LOBBY went from 16 free seats to 2 and
                    % stayed there, even though every welcomed member left again
                    % cleanly. Fourteen seats gone.
                    %
                    % The comment a few lines up has described this hole all
                    % along - "a letter carrying anything but a join, or a letter
                    % too damaged to decode at all, never reaches a handler - so
                    % no handler can give its seat back" - and named it as
                    % something the C# side got wrong. It was wrong HERE too. The
                    % code only ever returned a seat on a clean /leave or a reap,
                    % so an empty letter, a join refused because the name was
                    % taken, and a letter from a stranger each cost a seat for
                    % good. Under a burst that is most of them.
                    %
                    % ONE RULE, APPLIED TO EVERY LETTER: XROUT spent a seat to
                    % deliver this, so if the sender is not sitting in the room
                    % once we have finished with it, nobody is holding that seat
                    % and it must go straight back.
                    %
                    % This does NOT double-count with the /leave and reapDead
                    % paths. A leave arrives as an ORDINARY message, port to
                    % port, and never comes through this branch at all - only the
                    % JOIN is a letter. So each member's seat is returned exactly
                    % once, by whichever of the three applies.
                    %
                    % KNOWN GAP, said plainly rather than left to be discovered:
                    % a letter from somebody who IS already a member keeps its
                    % seat and should not. Clients send port to port after
                    % joining, so it does not arise in normal use, and guessing a
                    % fix for a case never seen would be worse than naming it.
                    % ---- ONLY A REAL LETTER SPENT A SEAT --------------------
                    % MEASURED, and it is the worst kind of bug: a FEEDBACK
                    % LOOP that this very block created. Four users joined and
                    % left, and CHAT-LOBBY went from 16 free seats to 3470 while
                    % the terminal filled with "DROP empty letter" for ever.
                    %
                    % Here is the loop. Returning a seat means sending XMPINFC
                    % to XROUT. XROUT ANSWERS, and its answer arrives back on
                    % this very port as a routed message with no chat payload -
                    % which the code above drops as an empty letter, and which
                    % this rule then read as "a letter whose sender is not a
                    % member", so it returned ANOTHER seat, which produced
                    % ANOTHER answer. The note at the foot of this file has
                    % warned since the first version that the XMPINFC reply
                    % "comes back later on our own port as an ordinary message"
                    % and that ignoring it was "safe by accident until somebody
                    % says so". It stopped being safe the moment this rule
                    % started acting on every routed arrival.
                    %
                    % THE DISCRIMINATOR IS BYTE 1. A client's letter carries the
                    % XSLET service number, 65. XROUT OVERWRITES that byte with
                    % a return status when it answers - the header comment in
                    % the receive loop says so, and it is the same test the C#
                    % load client uses to spot a refused letter. So a letter
                    % with 65 in byte 1 came from a client and spent a seat;
                    % anything else is XROUT talking to us and spent nothing.
                    IF letterService = XSLET THEN
                        findByMagic(senderMagic) =: nowMember
                        IF nowMember = 0 THEN
                            xmpinfc(0, myPort, 1, 0) =: returnStatus
                            IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
                                OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'SV: seat return failed')
                                OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                            ENDIF
                        ENDIF
                    ENDIF
                ELSE
                    % No header on this path: an ordinary message sent straight
                    % to our magic number is exactly the bytes the peer wrote.
                    handleMessage
                ENDIF
                ENDIF

                % ---- AND NOW BURY ANYBODY WHO TURNED OUT TO BE GONE ---------
                % One place, after everything the arrival caused has finished,
                % because that is the only depth at which outBuf is free. Both
                % branches above lead here on purpose: a member can be found
                % dead by any broadcast, and the routed and direct paths both
                % broadcast.
                IF deadFound >< 0 THEN
                    reapDead
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDDO
    ENDROUTINE

ENDMODULE

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% STILL NOT DONE, and worth saying rather than leaving to be met:
%
%  1. THIS ITEM USED TO READ "Nothing has been compiled", and it is kept here
%     in corrected form rather than deleted, because everything it worried about
%     has now been settled by the compiler itself rather than by argument:
%     INTEGER4, two-dimensional BYTES ARRAY, the implicit extra ARRAY keyword on
%     a BYTES declaration, BOOLEAN := TRUE, and the XMMSGIDENTIFIER and
%     XMUSERADDRESS names out of XMP-B02:IMPT. All of it compiles and runs.
%
%     The one thing the list did NOT anticipate is the one that cost the most:
%     a letter arrives with its XSLET header still in front of it. No amount of
%     checking declarations against a manual would have found that - only the
%     raw dump did.
%
%  2. The seat returned on Leave uses XMPINFC(flags, portNumber, extraConn,
%     serialNumber). Its reply comes back later on our own port as an ordinary
%     message, which handleMessage will see and not understand. Decide what to
%     do with it - ignoring an unknown kind is already the behaviour, so this
%     is safe, but it is safe by accident until somebody says so.
%
%  3. Each reason string carries its length as a separate constant, because
%     PLANC has no LENGTH routine and SIZE's meaning for a BYTES parameter is
%     not established. A string and its number must be changed together.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
